              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "In plastic welding, **nitrogen is used primarily to prevent oxidation** during the heating and bonding process. \n\nSpecifically, nitrogen acts as a shielding gas to create a much stronger, cleaner, and more durable weld compared to traditional hot-air welding.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Eliminating Oxidation (The Core Reason)\nRegular air is composed of approximately 78% nitrogen and 21% oxygen. When plastic is heated to its melting point using regular compressed air, the oxygen in the air rapidly reacts with the hot plastic. This process is called **oxidation**. \n* Oxidation degrades the polymer chains on a molecular level, preventing them from fusing together properly.\n* When nitrogen (an inert/non-reactive gas) is used, it displaces the oxygen around the weld zone, allowing the plastic to melt cleanly without any chemical degradation.\n\n### 2. Preventing Burning and Charring\nJust like a fire needs heat, fuel, and oxygen, plastic burning also requires oxygen. \n* **With Hot Air:** The combination of intense heat and oxygen causes the plastic to char, burn, discolor, and release thick smoke.\n* **With Nitrogen:** Because the oxygen is pushed away, the plastic cannot combust. It simply melts smoothly. There is no soot, charring, or ash introduced into the weld, ensuring a highly contaminated-free bond.\n\n### 3. Maximizing Weld Strength\nBecause the polymer chains are not degraded by oxygen, they can fully mix and cross-link with the plastic welding rod at a molecular level. This produces a weld that is nearly as strong as the original, unbroken plastic. Hot-air welds, by contrast, are often brittle and highly prone to cracking under stress due to the weakened, oxidized plastic structure.\n\n### 4. Safer Working Environment\nHeating plastics in the presence of oxygen creates combustion byproducts, leading to toxic fumes and heavy smoke. By eliminating oxygen, nitrogen plastic welding produces far fewer fumes and very little smoke, making the process much safer for the technician.\n\n### Which Plastics Benefit the Most?\nWhile nitrogen is ideal for almost all thermoplastics, it is **absolutely essential** for plastics that are highly sensitive to oxidation, such as:\n* **Polypropylene (PP)** (commonly used in automotive bumpers and trim)\n* **Polyethylene (PE)** (commonly used in water tanks, agricultural equipment, and kayak hulls)\n* **Nylon and Polyurethane** \n\n### Summary\nUsing nitrogen in plastic welding is highly analogous to using Argon gas in TIG/MIG welding on metals. It shields the molten material from the atmosphere to ensure a pure, strong, and professional-grade bond.",
